Overview
A data platform holds approximately 750,000 existing customer records that need to be created in ComplyAdvantage. These records will be segregated by entity type and exported as CSV files. The solution ingests these records through the existing Comply Adapter, applying the same business logic (existence check, then create/update with labelling and monitoring) that is applied to live CRM events today.
To achieve this, we introduce a new Loader service that converts CSV files to Avro objects and publishes to four new Kafka topics, and small changes to the Comply Adapter to consume from both existing and migration topics, enforce rate limiting via a Throttle EIP, and track failed records.

Current State: How the Adapter Works Today
The Comply Adapter (Apache Camel / Spring Boot) consumes from four Kafka topics — one per entity type ([adviser], [legal entity], [client], [licensee] — placeholders, to be confirmed).
Processing workflow for each record:

Call getCustomer to check whether the record exists in ComplyAdvantage.
If exists → call updateCustomer, then updateMonitor.
If not exists → call createCustomer, then updateLabel.

A single inbound Kafka message triggers 2–3 downstream API calls to ComplyAdvantage. This is an asynchronous, multi-step process.

Migration Requirement
The data platform will segregate 750,000 existing customer records by entity type, export each entity's records as a CSV file (each record carrying a unique identifier), and place the files in an SFTP location.
These records must flow through the Comply Adapter's existing workflow so that identical business logic (get → create/update → label/monitor) is applied to them, just as it is to live CRM events. All API responses (HTTP 200 and 4xx) must be captured for auditing and reconciliation.

Why Kafka for This Migration
Reusing the existing Kafka intake (rather than building a new API entry point) gives several advantages for a bulk migration of this size:

Asynchronous decoupling — the Loader publishes records as fast as it can read them, while the adapter consumes and processes at its own controlled pace. The producer and consumer never block each other, which suits a multi-step process where each record makes 2–3 downstream API calls.
Natural buffering — Kafka holds the migration records durably, absorbing the difference between fast file reading and rate-limited API calls. There is no need for the Loader to manage backpressure.
Built-in replay — because Kafka retains messages by offset, the migration can be replayed or resumed from any point without re-reading the source files, which is valuable if a run is interrupted.
Isolation via separate topics — using dedicated migration topics keeps the migration traffic completely separate from live CRM traffic, so the existing systems are unaffected and migration load can be paused or throttled independently.
Reuse of proven logic — the adapter already contains the full business workflow, error handling, and ComplyAdvantage integration. Kafka lets us feed migration records through that same, already-tested path with no business-logic change.


Proposed Solution
A new Loader service converts CSV files to Avro events and publishes them to four new, migration-only Kafka topics. The adapter is configured to consume from both the existing topics (CRM) and the migration topics, applying identical business logic to both. Rate limiting is enforced via the Apache Camel Throttle EIP, and the adapter tracks any failed records.
