Architecture & Implementation Details
The Loader Service
The Loader has a single, simple responsibility: read CSV files and publish them to Kafka. It does not track records or handle business errors — that is the adapter's job. Keeping the Loader thin makes it quick to build and easy to reason about.
For each row, the Loader:

Reads the CSV file from S3.
Validates the row at a basic structural level (mandatory fields present, parseable).
Maps CSV columns to the existing Avro schema fields.
For schema-mandatory fields not needed by ComplyAdvantage, sets default/sensible values (passed through unchanged, not sent to the API).
Builds the Avro object using POJOs generated from the existing .avsc (downloaded from the schema registry and generated at build time via the avro-maven-plugin).
Publishes the Avro event to the corresponding migration topic, using the record identifier as the Kafka message key.

Entity name, S3 prefix, and target topic are externalized as configuration, so the same Loader runs once per entity.
Hosting: The Loader is deployed to the existing AWS EKS cluster — the same platform our services already run on — rather than introducing a new compute service. This keeps it within familiar, stable operational tooling. It runs as a short-lived job (e.g. a Kubernetes Job) per entity, since its work is finite.
Changes to the Comply Adapter
The adapter requires three changes:

Topic consumption — update the Kafka consumer configuration to subscribe to both the existing topic and its migration counterpart (e.g. adviser and adviser.migration). The internal routing and business logic remain unchanged.
Rate limiting — add the Apache Camel Throttle EIP on the route segment that calls the ComplyAdvantage Mesh API, to cap the outbound call rate.
Failed-record tracking — when a record fails (API error, timeout, unrecoverable error), the adapter logs the error (default behaviour) and additionally records the failure — keyed by the record identifier from the message — to a tracking store, so failures can be queried and reported. The adapter continues consuming subsequent messages; one failed record does not stop the migration.

New Migration Topics
Four new Kafka topics are created (adviser.migration, client.migration, licensee.migration, legalEntity.migration). These reuse the existing Avro schemas, registered under new Schema Registry subjects via TopicNameStrategy (e.g. adviser.migration-value), with no serializer configuration changes.
The existing four topics remain untouched, so other systems consuming them are unaffected. Migration traffic is fully isolated and can be paused or replayed independently.

Rate Limiting
ComplyAdvantage enforces a per-account rate limit. Exceeding it returns HTTP 429 (API rate limit exceeded). Responses include Ratelimit-Limit, Ratelimit-Remaining, and Ratelimit-Reset headers.
Throttle EIP Configuration
The Apache Camel Throttle EIP caps the outbound Mesh API call rate on the adapter route. This is the primary control because it limits actual API calls, accounting for the 2–3 calls each record generates.
50 calls per second:
java.throttle(50).timePeriodMillis(1000)
30 calls per 10-second window:
java.throttle(30).timePeriodMillis(10000)
The value can also be header/expression-driven so it is tunable at runtime without redeployment:
java.throttle(header("throttleValue")).timePeriodMillis(1000)
Since both BAU (CRM) and migration traffic flow through the same route, a single throttle enforces the limit across both streams combined.

Dependency: ComplyAdvantage's per-account rate limit is not publicly documented. We must confirm the configured limit with our account manager before finalising the Throttle EIP value and migration run windows. Each record triggers 2–3 calls, so the confirmed limit drives total run duration.


Response Capture & Reconciliation
All Mesh API responses (HTTP 200 and 4xx) must be captured for monitoring and reconciliation. The adapter logs each ComplyAdvantage response (including record identifier and HTTP status); a CloudWatch Logs subscription filter streams logs via Kinesis Firehose into an S3 audit bucket; and Athena queries the S3 data to produce the reconciliation report — HTTP 200 vs 4xx counts, breakdown of 4xx reasons, and submitted-vs-completed tally per entity.

Prerequisite: The adapter's logs must include a record identifier and HTTP status code per call. To be verified.


Field Mapping
The ComplyAdvantage Mesh API requires only ~15–20 fields. The platform's Kafka schema carries 100+ fields (an estimated 20–30 are mandatory). For this migration: fields required by the Mesh API are mapped from the source CSV; schema-mandatory fields not needed by the Mesh API are populated with default/sensible values (passed through unchanged but not forwarded to the API); optional schema fields are omitted if not present in the source.
A detailed field-mapping specification will be produced during the implementation phase.

Alternative Considered – REST API Intake on the Adapter
We considered adding a REST API entry point to the adapter (it is currently Kafka-only) and pushing records through that API instead.
Why this was not chosen: the per-record workflow is asynchronous with 2–3 internal API calls, so a synchronous REST API would need a new contract, request validation, authentication, and a job-status/tracking mechanism to report multi-step outcomes. All of that is new code and regression testing effort disproportionate to this migration activity. Reusing Kafka keeps the change minimal.

Open question for the EA: Independent of this migration, is there strategic value in giving the adapter an API intake (e.g. for ad-hoc, manual onboarding outside the CRM event flow)? If so, we can scope and size it as a separate roadmap item.


